 <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold">Step 4: Replace estimates as quotes arrive</h2>
 <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-slate-700">Update each line when you receive a real price. Keep the 
date of every quote and note what is included, so you can compare providers fairly and spot what is 
missing. The goal is a decision-ready budget, not false precision.</p>
 <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold">Using the calculator for a first draft</h2>
 <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-slate-700">The <a class="font-semibold text-sky-700 underline" 
href="/calculator">calculator</a> gives you a planning estimate for the move from the states, distance, 
home size and move type you choose. The fields under “Other relocation costs” match the buckets above: 
travel, temporary housing, deposits and setup, and packing or extras. Enter the amounts you already know
and leave the rest at zero until you have a figure.</p>
 <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-slate-700">To see how a specific route compares across move types, 
open a route guide such as <a class="font-semibold text-sky-700 underline" href="/moving-cost/ca-to-tx">California to Texas</a>, or browse <a class="font-semibold text-sky-700 underline" href="/moving-cost">all route guides</a>. To understand how the estimate is built, read the <a class="font-semibold 
text-sky-700 underline" href="/methodology">methodology</a>.</p>
